A number of competitors have 64 bit CHIPS. The problem is that their OSs
are all still 32 bits. Maybe they can have several processors addressing
different 4GB chunks of memory, but expanding memory access to 64 bits is
a major undertaking. Eventually others'll get there, and till then they'll
talk about their chips and elide the fact that no single processor can
address more than 32 bits worth of memory in their OSs. That may take
them several years though. Also given the history of Sun in causing user
apps to have to be rewritten/junked (remember the SunOS -> Solaris "migration"
and how statically linked apps could not be run???), who's to say that
similar things won't happen when they switch to 64 bits in their OS? (The
"rebuild your kernel" disease is something I've been told is common in
unix-land and may very well infect HP, IBM, etc. in similar fashion.)

Getting an OS to behave sensibly in 64 bits is astonishingly pervasive as
a problem. Of course you can cheap out...as rumor has it M$ is doing...
and provide only a way to map address areas in 64 bit space and do all
access via routines that copy to a low memory buffer or from it. You can
even make it sort of look like I/O is possible, but what you're talking is
more akin to a memory virtual disk than really giving useful 64 bit access
then. Bragging rights, more or less, but nothing really that anyone would want
to use...just a way to have hype that isn't totally thin air.

Doing 64 bits right means I/O direct to the space, program access direct to
the space, even being able to page, swap, execute etc. there. Pointers
get to be longer, and an amazing number of internal structures have to be
carefully altered. And very little of the work shows except when you look
"under the hood"; the glitz factor is small. The performance gains possible
with such systems are, however, at times quite large. Remember the business
about joins running 250 times faster? Simple minded hype-bait doesn't get
that for you. The real stuff under the hood does.

'Nuff said.
glenn everhart



