Er, look at Alpha VMS 7.x (7.1 is better than 7.0 at this in various
details but 7.0 has the concept.) I/O to p2 addresses is in there on
exactly the same terms as everything else. Mapping operations depend on
the hardware interconnect of the I/O device of course; for a large
class of same there is no mapping needed...the routines charged with
doing it are functionally no-ops there.

Check out how OVMS handles pointers and structures with them also...old
sizes work fine, things don't need to grow, and old user mode executables
keep on working. But progs wanting direct access to 64 bit space,
just use the 64 bit formats.

Re Ron@sensor.com's comment about how compatibility will happen, note
that OVMS binaries from V1.x still run on 7.1 if user mode only. Priv'd
interfaces may change but keeping from breaking user mode stuff has
been strongly supported. VERY unlike Sun et alia.

Yes, a 32 bit machine can use mapping to address more space...pdp11's
do that, have for decades...but have to fiddle with the space. I've written
ODL files with 5 cotrees and hundreds of leaves, and programmed that
way, but when I was able to stop doing it, I was amazed how much grief
I left behind. The idea of going back to that, in order to gain 64 bit
access, is not the kind of thing I consider rational.



