In article <karki.851720392@spot.Colorado.EDU>, karki@spot.Colorado.EDU (KARKI JITENDRA BAHADUR) writes:

:1) Does DEC publish an equivalent to Intel's AP-500 or AP-526 for their
:21164 processor?  These pamphlets are really handy for estimating the
:speed of hand-optimized processor bound code sequences, and I can't seem
:to get the info I need to do this from DEC's ftp site.  In particular, a
:table indicating which unit the various instructions go to is essential
:for making such an assessment (e.g. can one execute a floating point
:load or store on the same clock cycle as a floating point add and
:multiply are simultaneously issued?)

   You want the microprocessor handbooks.  These are available from
   Digital's semiconductor folks for various (announced) Alpha
   microprocessors.  (See the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions
   monthly posting for the DECchip hotline telephone number, or see
   the www.semiconductor.digital.com web site...)

   Bluntly, hand-tuning the code is likely a waste of time -- the Alpha
   compilers are quite good at this, at code scheduling, and at dealing
   with tuning the code for future generations of processors.  (Current
   generation Alpha microprocessors have two integer and two floating
   point units.  That will change.)  I would code in, say, C, and then
   profile the code for the hottest routines, and _then_ see if you can
   hand-tune better than the compilers' code generators.  (And if you
   can, Digital's compiler folks would be interested in knowing more
   about the situation, too.)

:3) Why don't alpha system vendors specify exactly which processor is in
:each of their offerings?  This may just be a PC thing, but with pricing
:competitive with high-end PCs, one might think it would be a good idea
:to emulate this aspect of PC marketing strategy.

