jfmezei <nospam.jfmezei@videotron.ca> wrote:
>re: all the new Alphas that DEC is rumoured to unleash in 1997.
>
>What about competitors' 64 bit chips (ultra Sparc?). Will these not
>eventually compete head to head with Alpha in terms of
>price-performance?

First thing said chips need to compete on an equal footing is a 64-bit OS 
and 64-bit apps. Sun and HP have neither. SGI does have a 64-bit OS (Irix) 
but has not concentrated on the commercial 64-bit market.

Secondly, the 21264 chip will allow DEC to offer at least twice the 
performance of any of its rivals. Sustained performance leadership is the 
least of DEC's worries right now.

>
>It is very nice to know that DEC will be coming out with fast chips, but
>since DEC has consistently been unable to gain wide market acceptance in
>the years that its ALPHA has been around, and now that DEC will loose
>its "we are the only ones with 64 bit chips" edge, I really wonder
>if 1997 will be any different from previous years.

I believe that DEC still has another year as the only vendor with a 
compelete (chip, OS, apps, compilers, migration strategy) solution. 
64-bits has not yet become a mainstream issue; this will change late next 
year when Microsoft delivers 64-bit WNT.

Alpha in the past has been unable to gain widespread market acceptance 
because it targeted niche markets. Given the substantial decrease in Alpha 
prices over the last six months--and forthcoming price-performance 
improvements as well--I believe that Alpha is poised to become much more 
successful in 97.


