I have an old Starion 400 (bought 11/94), and have been using the Intel
Overdrive set at 133 MHz ever since the chip came on the market.

Setting the motherboard speed to 66 MHz is a snap.

I don't know why Intel lists it as incompatible.  There must be some
slight difference in the BIOS or motherboard design between some serial
numbers.

Anyway, I can attest to the fact that it's worked just fine in my machine
for many, many months.

If you change Starion motherboards, you'll have to change cases, too. 
When I wanted to buy extra cache for my machine, I discovered it was only
available from DEC, and at $85/128k.  The motherboards will probably be
similarly overpriced, and you'd be better off buying a modern,
high-performance motherboard, chip and case, and taking it from there.


