SHANNON KNOWS DEC'S FEARLESS PROGNOSTICATIONS FOR 1997

(c) 1996 by Terry C. Shannon, Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC

It's that time again. SKD once again has assembled its crack team of 
clairvoyant canines, sentient soothsayers, and 900-number psychics to 
conjure up the third annual edition of our ever-popular and 
usually-accurate Fearless Prognostications. We had a tough time culling 
our myriad findings down to a more manageable number, but once we 
eliminated references to Roswell, Elvis, and Microsoft's hostile takeover 
of the US Government, we selected the following worthy finalists for our 
Top Ten List for 1997.

10) A New Window on TurboLaser

Speculation about WNT on TurboLaser has been rife for some time, and both 
Bill Gates and Bob Palmer recently confirmed the forthcoming availability 
of same. FCS is slated for the March '97 timeframe. Maximum supported 
configurations could range as high as 12 CPUS; whether WNT-based systems 
will be offered at a discount remains to be seen.

9) Alpha Econoboxes Emerge

Alpha performance grew by more than 70 percent last year, while cost of 
performance took a nosedive. Last July, DEC unveiled a 433 MHz chip that 
outperformed the 400 MHz incumbent by 20 percent, yet cost 25 percent 
less than the 400 MHz part. Last month, DEC slashed the prices of EV5 and 
EV56 chips by 40 to 50 percent. Given the near-term availability of 
sub-$3K Alpha systems based on the DEC/Mitsubishi 21164PC chip and the 
Samsung chip implementation, Alpha desktops at popular prices are a sure 
bet. DEC itself may get into the act with an Alpha econobox.

8) Dreaming in double-time

Compaq, HP, et al are getting into the WNT personal workstation biz in a 
real big way. Accordingly, DEC will virtually double the 
price/performance of its Alpha Personal Workstation lineup. The Alpha 300 
and 366 boxes are slated to be replaced with 433a and 500a Miata systems 
whose entry prices should be about $5K and $12K respectively.

7) TurboLaser Turbocharged

Expected in 1H97 is an EV56-based TurboLaser whose clock speed will 
exceed 600 MHz. Since DEC Semiconductor late last month informed SKD that 
there are no current plans for a 633 MHz part (not enough performance 
delta vis-a-vis the-600 MHz EV56 chip?), we expect an even faster 
TurboLaser. A 667 MHz CPU? Stay tuned. And don't rule out the 633 MHz 
chip, either.

6) Rawhide Refresh

Look for DEC to boost the performance of the AlphaServer 4100 with a 467 
MHZ EV56 CPU. EV6 support will be extended to Rawhide, but probably not 
before early FY98.

5) Debut of the Dreamserver

DEC this year will debut "Dreamserver" analogs to its Intel/Alpha 
Personal Workstations. The new servers will be based on a common platform 
which will be differentiated solely by its Intel or Alpha CPUs. Alpha 
itself will gain new differentiation late this year when Microsoft begins 
shipping 64-bit WNT V5.0.

4) EV6 Makes for a Red Hot Summer

The third-generation Alpha microprocessor is likely to appear in systems 
sometime this summer. If past is prologue, the 21264 chip will first 
appear in top-of-the-line workstations, then some months later in 
high-end enterprise servers. SKD estimates that the 21264 could deliver 
up to 40 SPECint95 and 60 SPECfp95.

3) Validating the Volume Proposition

According to Digital Semiconductor, 1997 is the year that Alpha will 
start to assert a presence in the mainstream. Despite the availability of 
low-cost Alpha chipsets and FX!32 translation technology, Alpha faces an 
uphill battle in its effort to gain widespread acceptance. SKD believes 
that $2.5K Alpha desktops will be more appealing to users running CAD, 
graphics, and animation apps than to users running personal productivity 
software. 

On the other hand, sales of StrongARM chips will soar during the coming 
year as additional Internet appliance and thin client vendors opt for a 
"StrongARM-inside" strategy. StrongARM also has a good shot at the 
embedded systems marketplace, not to mention great potential as the 
engine that powers cellular telephones, PDAs, and other consumer 
electronic products. 

(2) Alpha Installed Base Burgeons

According to SKD's historically-accurate estimates, the Alpha installed 
base will top the quarter-million mark by mid-97. By year's end, over 
300,000 Alpha systems--representing well over $15B in revenue--are likely 
to be in the field

1) R. Palmer and Digital Equipment: Tanned, not toasted.

DEC's 4FQ96 and 1FQ97 financial results interrupted six consecutive 
quarters of profitability and revenue growth. Despite these setbacks, the 
firm is well positioned for resurgence in CY97. Two years ago, DEC 
successfully executed an against-all-odds recovery from its 3FQ94 "Ides 
of April" nadir. Today's situation is less serious, and DEC is addressing 
the underlying causes. As for Mr. Palmer, the term "Comeback Kid" seems 
apropos for politicians and CEOs alike.

