In article <19961224201000.PAA15214@ladder01.news.aol.com>, larx101@aol.com (LarX101) writes:
:I have an AlphaStation 400/233 running OpenVMS (I forget the version)
..
:The problem I have is, the highest speed I
:can get out of the port for the terminal is 9600.  My manual states that
:the system's highest serial speed is 38400.  If my console is set for
:serial from the bios, is 9600 the limit? 

   The console serial line itself operates exclusively at 9600bps.

   The serial lines on the system can be set for baud rates up to 38.4Kbps
   via "SET TERMINAL/SPEED=<br>/PERMANENT <ddcu:>" DCL commands.

   Running at 38.4Kbps limits the cable length to about four meters, and
   then only with good quality cables.

   The serial console line is not traditionally intended for heavy-duty
   point-to-point communications or networking, it's intended for connection
   to the system console terminal.  (eg: there are certain critical OpenVMS
   operational messages sent only to the system console terminal -- pagefile
   warnings, among these...)

