stuttjc@eng.auburn.edu (James C. Stutts) wrote on 17 Dec 1996 07:53:38
GMT:

>....  My question is this: is there a problem finding drivers for 
>boards and peripherals for NT on Alpha?  Is that a limitation on the system?  

Depending on the boards and peripherals you intend to use, this
*might* be a limitation. However, most vendors providing drivers for
Windows NT do so for all platforms. 

To find out whether a specific piece of hardware is supported, first
check Microsoft's HCL (hardware compatibility list) for Windows NT
which mentions on which hardware architecture a given board or
peripheral is supported. If you can't find it there, the individual
vendor's home page is usually the best source of information.

>...What improvements
>(besides the touted performance gain) will be added in FX!32?

It let's you execute almost all applications offered for Windows NT on
the Intel platform. If a 32-bit application (or shareware) you want to
use is not available as a 'native' Alpha application (i.e. compiled on
an Alpha), you can nevertheless use it on your Alpha based Windows NT
system.

FX!32 will sort of 'translate' the Intel binaries to Alpha binaries on
the fly, collect information on specific modules and store optimized
Alpha versions of these modules for later use (when you invoke the
application again). This opens the door to a wealth of 32-bit
applications not (yet) available on the Alpha platform.

