|>Steve has a good point. If you want to be sure that a system/design can
|>accomodate a future Intel technology, make sure that the system you
|>purchase can accomodate it during your original purchase decision process.

This often requires a talent for predicting the future - which has repeatedly
proven itself to be difficult where the issue of Intel upgrades is concerned.
Intel touted the future Pentium OverDrive upgradeability of 486 systems and
got manufacturers to make claims about that well in advance of anyone seeing
an OverDrive chip.  When they did show up, oops, they weren't quite as
compatible as Intel had claimed.

The same goes for the faster Pentiums - starting with the 120MHz model, Intel
added a new "clock multiplier input" - if your motherboard didn't supply it,
you couldn't use the new chips (but instead had to wait for the OverDrive
version.)  They've also changed voltages several times, and now have yet
another processor socket type on the latest revisions.

I think it's unfair to blame a manufacturer, or a buyer, for not being privy
to Intel's future plans.  I think it's reasonable to be able to verify that
if your prospective purchase doesn't have the current top-end CPU that you
can upgrade to that CPU later, but things get MUCH stickier when you want to
predict compatibility with unannounced future chips.
