In article <59998l$6i8@mars.hyperk.com>, Kevin Handy <kth@srv.net> wrote:
>If you use the simulated TM drive, you probibly need to do a
>'SET CPU 18B' (At least you need this to use it with RSTS/E,
>which I was just curious if it would run). Caused the RSTS/E
>operating system to crash if used in 22bit mode.

This is a common problem with Unibus devices connected to
a 22-bit Q-bus processor, whether the devices and processor be actual
hardware or emulated hardware.

There are three ways to solve this problem:

1.  Implement a Unibus map.  One of the Able Qniverters has this
feature, and it ought to be possible to add it to the emulator.  Although
I'm not thoroughly familiar with the emulator's source code, I suspect
that adding a Unibus map option would require a lot of tedious
recoding, and very likely a performance degradation when doing I/O.  
A question for Bob Supnik: have you considered this
as an option?  At least some of the older Unix drivers expect
there to always be a Unibus map.

B.  Implement a 22-bit capable tape drive device in the
emulator, such as a TSV05.  TMSCP emulation (as in a TU81 or
TK50) would also be possible, but would be a major headache!

III.  "Extend" your emulated Unibus device so it has the 4 extra
address bits.  Would almost certainly require rewriting the device
drivers that the emulated -11 runs.  Often, though, these extended
drivers already exist on the DECUS SIG tapes.  See, for example,

ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/
      pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11/rt/decus/11s109/22bit_dev

for examples of how the RX02 and RK06/07 devices were extended by
several manufacturers to have 22-bit addressing capability.

Technically, options B and III above are not mutually exclusive.
Indeed, DEC extended the TS11 registers to hold the 4 extra address
bits themselves.  (Well, very likely Dilog did it first, but at
least DEC formally adopted this extension - not surprising, considering
they were relabeling Dilog hardware and selling it at the time.)

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



