Przemek Klosowski wrote:
> 
> Yeah, but for marketing reasons DIGITAL announced that they will
> produce two versions of Alpha chips---one for Unix/VMS, costing more
> money, and another, cheaper, for NT.

Where and when did Digital announce this?
I follow their product announcements fairly closely, and have never seen
anything like this.

> The chips are identical
> (stepping, clock.  etc) except for one odd hardware/system register,
> missing in the cheaper version, that prevents mapping full 64 bits of
> address 
1. Current implementations of the Alpha chip only map 43 bits of virtual
address.
The current 3 level page mapping allows up to 55 bits of VA, but only
with increased page size. 
2. The Alpha uses software to handle TLB misses, so the virtual to
physical mapping is entirely soft, and is handled by PAL code.
> (this is from memory, so I may have misremembered the details).
This seems very likely!
> 
> NT is not affected---it is 32-bit anyway.
> 
The principal difference between NT-only systems and those capable of
running all three supported operating systems is the console firmware.
NT uses the ARC console, while VMS and UNIX use a different console
subsystem. The NT-only systems do not include the VMS/UNIX console.
(They may also be missing the VMS PAL code, but that is irrelevent since
you can't boot VMS without its console code anyway.)

