organization: University of Colorado
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



I believe that the price disparity is due to internal pricing, not
physical differences.

From what I've been told, the development cost of Digital Unix and VMS
is charged to the workstations group. Digital Semiconductor doesn't
charge the UNIX/VMS "tax" on parts it sells. Thus, resellers (who
e.g., buy PC164 system from Digital Semi) aren't hit with that tax.

This is why you can get cheap Linux, W/NT and (sometimes) Unix systems
through resellers.

In the future, there will be a revised pricing model that allegedly
will make Digital Unix much cheaper. However, I don't know the details
about this.


-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



