In article <32B19468.150D@east.sun.com>, Miki Sarkozi SSE Pittsburgh <miki.sarkozi@east.sun.com> writes:

>1) what is the difference between the following machines, ie.,
>which is better, faster, preferable to have....?

Graphics adapters and packageing mostly.  I'll reorder the list
slightly.

>       Microvax II

The first MicroVAX I was a multi-board affair that, with a tail wind,
might have been as fast as an 11/730 (~.3 of an 11/780).  It came in
two floating point options where the default was probably the floating
format least used by customers.

The MicroVAX II was the follow-on system that reduced the core of
VAX system to a single chip (instead of spread over two boards).
The CPU board was had lots other glue chips, but some of these
were console interface, I/O interface, etc.  For raw CPU speed
the system was about as the VAX 11/780.  Compared to the MicroVAX
I the improvment was astonishing.  The MicroVAX II was packaged
in various configurations.

>	vaxstation II

Take a MicroVAX II in the tall, narrow pedistal cabinet and add
a dumb frame buffer for a graphics adapter.  Under ULTRIX put a
version of X10 on it for the windowing software and you have the
ULTRIX version of the VAXstation II.

>       vaxstation II/GPX

The problem with dumb frame buffer is that rely on the CPU to
do all the interesting work of graphics operations.  The GPX
board set was a 4-8 plane graphics subsystem with hardware
acceleration support for selected operations.  Typical configurations
where 4 plane gray-scale and 8 plane color.  At the same time
the VAXstation II/GPX systems were released a new box for it
was also released that had more I/O capacity, space more disks
and a tape in the cabinet and easy to service.  It was conviently
on wheels and was sturdy enough to sit on.  The BA123 was a considerable
improvement over the BA23 pedistal.

>	vaxstation II/RC

Take Q-bus backplane in a VAXstation II BA23 subsystem and put
epoxy in half the slots.  You now have a system of very limited
expansion capacity that we believed could sell for less money.
I guess we sold more than a few...  It is otherwise the same
as a system with the same parts, but without the epoxy.

>2) Can Ultrix be loaded on any of all of these and can Ultrix
>be found on the net somewhere or is this purchasable from
>someone?

ULTRIX has support for all of these systems.  You can buy a
license and distribution from Digital Equipment Corporation
for probably more than the system are worth.  I don't know
why it costs so much, but I'd guess it is so that we don't
lose money just by selling a license.

