lionel@quark.zko.dec.com (Steve Lionel) writes...
}<miki.sarkozi@east.sun.com> writes:
}|>1) what is the difference between the following machines, ie.,
}|>which is better, faster, preferable to have....?
}|>
}|>	vaxstation II
}|>	vaxstation II/RC
}|>	vaxstation II/GPX
}|>	Microvax II
} 
}The first three are "workstation" versions of the MicroVAX II, in that they
}include a graphics processor and keyboard/mouse interface.  The /RC is a
}"Reduced Cost" configuration with limited expandability.  (The limitation
}is caused by having some option slots filled with epoxy, leading to the
}observation that "RC" means "Resin Coated".)  The /GPX is a variant with a
}faster/better graphics processor, so this would be the most desireable.
}The MicroVAX II is not a graphics-based system and would require an external
}serial terminal to be attached.  All are based on the same processor chip
}(MicroVAX II) and are the same speed, that being about .9 of a VAX-11/780.  
}They are all QBUS-based systems with a memory limit of (if I recall correctly) 
}14MB.
}Steve Lionel                      mailto:lionel@quark.zko.dec.com

That'd be 16MB for the MicroVAX II - we have one with that much - and
I assume that the VAXstation IIs have the same limit (which might not
be all that good of an assumption, particularly for the RC flavor).

I expect you were confusing them with the slightly newer, and smaller,
MicroVAX/VAXstation 2000 systems, which do have a 14MB limit.

And then there's the upgraded MicroVAX/VAXstation II systems, the MicroVAX
and VAXstation III, 3+, and VAX/VAXstation 4.  I'm not sure what the regular
3 variety had for a limit, byt the 3+ and 4 systems have either 32MB or 48MB
depending on which cabinet you have (32MB in BA23, 48MB in BA123).  These
also ahve the advantage of being a lot faster.  The 3+ is a 3.8VUP system
and the 4 is a 5.0VUP system (about 5.5 times faster than the basic microVAX
II).  Note that neither the 3+ or 4 upgrade uses the same memory as the II -
MS650 vs. MS630.  The VAX/VAXstation 4 also includes a DSSI controller on the
CPU module (which is basically a VAX 4000-200).

For those who are inclined to play with such things, I would think that a
VAXstation 4/GPX would be fairly nice if you can find one (although I
personally would much rather have an AlphaStation 500/500).

--- Carl



