Subject: Old Starion (400, 500 series)
Date: 1996/12/17
Message-ID: <19961217185500.NAA09510@ladder01.news.aol.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 204583898
organization: AOL http://www.aol.com
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
x-admin: news@aol.com


Anyone who's using one of the old Starion series (i.e., the 400 and 500
series) should be aware of two facts:

(1)  Despite what you might uncover in the Intel site, the Intel OverDrive
120MHz/133MHz processor works just fine in my Starion 500.  Just re-set
the motherboard speed to 66MHz, and plop the chip in.  Works just fine.

(2)  Digital Equipment has a BIOS upgrade for the Phoenix 4.04G that came
on our machines...it upgrades it to 4.04I.  The only thing I can spot that
the PHLASH upgrade changes is caching of the system ROM.  The original
BIOS is set up to cache the system ROM and doesn't allow any input from
the user.  The BIOS upgrade eliminates this, and I saw an immediate effect
in the performance of the disk and video subsystems when running under
Windows95.

My hard drive performance under Windows95 jumped from 1.1megs/sec
(uncached) to 2.0megs/sec.  Video throughput at 1024X768X64Kcolor jumped
from 7.1 million pixels/second to 9.3 million pixels/sec.

It's worth looking into!

I had to get some telephone help from DEC for the PHLASH upgrade.



