In article <32B6FD5B.41C6@loomcom.com>,
Seth Morabito  <sethm@loomcom.com> wrote:
>I'm very curious about how the demo software disk images were 
>made. Specifically, how were the RL and RK disk images for Unix
>version 5, 6, and 7 produced?  The more detailed the answer,
>the more points you will be awarded when the final score
>is tallied.  Partial credit will be given.  ;-)

An easy way to make a RL or RK disk image is to mount the
pack in your drive (I'll assume unit plug #1), and then do something like:

$MOU/FOR DLA1:
$COPY DLA1: RL01.IMAGE

if you're running VMS, or something like

.COPY/DEV/FILE DL1: RL01.IMA

if using RT-11, or something like

%dd if=/dev/rrl1a of=rl01.image

under a Unix.  (The raw device name above is valid under 2.11BSD; I'm
not sure what would be used in other implementations.)

I believe that RK05 support under VMS ended some time ago, but
I've been told that the old drivers still work (even though they
aren't being updated.)  RL01/02 support under VMS still works fine.

Please note that under RT-11 there are some odd block remappings
taking place in the RL02 driver, that may royally screw you over if
you are making an image of a pack from some OS other than RT-11.

I've used the above methods to make images of RT-11, RSX-11, and
various Unix disk packs with no problems other than the RT-11
caveat I mention above.

Bob Supnik's emulator also does TM11/TU10 emulation well, so
it ought to be possible to have it boot a magtape image, and build
bootable disks from there.  I don't think this was the method used.
Part of the difficutly when using this method with a 2.9 BSD
distribution tape is that the tape drivers there expect a Unibus
map to be present - something that doesn't exist in the current
version of Bob's emulator, which emulates a 11/73.  I'm not sure
if the System 5-7 tape drivers automatically expect a Unibus map
to be present.

