"Dave Rudder" <drig@magicweb.com> wrote on Mon, 16 Dec 1996 15:53:12
-0800:

>Hello,
>	I recently got my new AlphaServer 1000 and I'm trying to install Windows
>NT 4.0  I have the Microsoft Developer's Network CD-ROM.  I've
>successfully installed Digital Unix and RedHat Linux 3.0.3.  I have only
>this one Alpha and it doesn't have Windows on it yet.  I do, however, have
>a number of Intel Windows boxes available to me.  
>	My problem:  How do I make the boot disks.  The SRM console doesn't want
>to boot from the CD.  I was able to make the Linux boot disks on an Intel
>box, so I know the floppies are compatible.  But, any executable on the CD
>I try to run doesn't work.  I guess their all Alpha executables.  How do I
>boot NT?

You can't boot / install Windows NT from the SRM console. At the SRM
console prompt, type >>>set os_type WNT (or WindowsNT) and power cycle
the machine. It should come up with the ARC console (I believe the
AlphaServer 1000 has both ROM chips on board - otherwise you need to
upgrade... hmmm, sidegrade ? ...the firmware first.

The ARC (or AlphaBIOS) console lets you install Windows NT from the
CD-ROM.

Hope this helps,
Hans Bachner.

PS: to install Windows NT 3.5, you needed to physically disconnect (at
least the power plug of) the Unix (or OpenVMS) system disk. Otherwise,
the installation claimed that it had detected a 'virus' on the system
and cancelled the installation. Don't know whether this changed for
Windows NT 4.0, though... Once you have Windows NT installed, there's
no problem to upgrade or install additional NT variants even with the
'foreign' disk connected.

