In article <ALDERSON.96Dec13164526@netcom16.netcom.com>,
Richard M. Alderson III <alderson@netcom.com> wrote:
>>The QIC drive could be a TU-58.  It looks like a very small slow disk to the
>>software.  It is connected via a serial port and is probably useless.
>
>A TU-58 looks like a slightly pregnant 4mm DAT tape, not a 1/4" QIC.  Were they
>really used in anything other than HSC-50's?

Actually a TU58K is a 1/4" tape, a DC2000 with DEC's copyrighted ultra-lame
format on it, which works with QIC-40 and QIC-80 (although only at 2/3 the
capacity of the longer DC2120).  But it's not a DC600A, which is all a lot
of people picture when they hear "QIC".

Anyway the TU58 was also used for microcode loading/patching and/or diags
on the VAX-11/730, VAX-11/750, and PDP-11/44 (i.e. it came standard with those
models), and it was the main mass storage device on the PDT-11/130 (and VT103
too right?).  Nasty little drives but at ~$1800 they were cheap by DEC
standards, and it was definitely a good idea to have one medium that they
were sure all their customers could read.

John Wilson



