mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


The DECpc 433W has a Texas Instruments Graphics Architecture (TIGA)
based video controller chip (TMS34020? TMS34010?) from 90'/91'.

It can handle VGA (640x480) or 1280x1024 resolutions, and nothing in
between. Though, it has support for the IBM 8514/A programming interface
(it isn't register compatible with the 8514/A, however, so very few
pieces of software actually supports it).

The machine has a switch at the back near the video connector, which
is used to specify what kind of monitor is attached to the system.

The monitors sold with these machines (or the so called IGC, Intelligent
Graphics Controller a.k.a. Appian Rendition II or III, which were EISA
board variants sold around that same time by Digital and some others)
require a fixed frequency monitor that can handle "synch on green" (instead
of using separate signal cables).

You might be able to find relatively cheaply 19" or 20"/21" Digital 
monitors such as the VRT19, VRT21, VR320, etc. (as they're generally
troublesome to use with today's video adapters in general; search
http://altavista.digital.com for "fixed frequency monitor" or "Mirage
Storm" to find out more).

