In article <32B19468.150D@east.sun.com>, Miki Sarkozi SSE Pittsburgh 
<miki.sarkozi@east.sun.com> writes:

|>1) what is the difference between the following machines, ie.,
|>which is better, faster, preferable to have....?
|>
|>	vaxstation II
|>	vaxstation II/RC
|>	vaxstation II/GPX
|>	Microvax II

The first three are "workstation" versions of the MicroVAX II, in that they
include a graphics processor and keyboard/mouse interface.  The /RC is a
"Reduced Cost" configuration with limited expandability.  (The limitation
is caused by having some option slots filled with epoxy, leading to the
observation that "RC" means "Resin Coated".)  The /GPX is a variant with a
faster/better graphics processor, so this would be the most desireable.
The MicroVAX II is not a graphics-based system and would require an external
serial terminal to be attached.  All are based on the same processor chip
(MicroVAX II) and are the same speed, that being about .9 of a VAX-11/780.  
They are all QBUS-based systems with a memory limit of (if I recall correctly) 
14MB.

|>2) Can Ultrix be loaded on any of all of these and can Ultrix
|>be found on the net somewhere or is this purchasable from
|>someone?

Yes, if you can find VAX ULTRIX (not too likely nowadays), it will run on
any of these systems.  You might be better off with FreeBSD or NetBSD.  See
http://www.bitcon.no/~gunnar/vaxbsd/FAQ.html for info on NetBSD.

