> I've never seen adjacent suburban house addresses vary by less than
> 4 units (e.g. 1405, 1409...).  It's always 4 or 6.  Why is this?

So that you can build new houses between existing houses, or convert a
house into multiple dwelling units, without using fractional or
letter-suffixed addresses.

Curiously, the US Post Service sometimes does not want addresses to be
numbered monotonically.  I built a new house, and my assumption that I
should insert the new mailbox to preserve monotonic ordering caused
some protest.  It seems that USPS has their own internal sort order,
which is related to the order in which a mail carrier actually visits
the houses from block to block on their route.  Making changes to the
internal order is apparently a non-trivial process.

Let's see ... that would be a block-structured walk of nodes in a
non-obvious canonical order.  Are we back on topic yet?  :-)

  -=- Andrew Klossner (andrew@pogo.wv.tek.com)



