Rob Brown <robbrown@supernet.ab.ca> wrote:
>
>
>Bill Bradford <mrbill@texas.net> wrote in article 
>> purchased), but it appears to be OEMed by "Spartin Systems, Houston, TX",
>> according to the name badge on the front.... Instead of a floppy drive,
>> it has a Sun-3-style 1/4" QIC tape drive..... I havent had much chance
>
>The QIC drive could be a TU-58.  It looks like a very small slow disk to
>the software.  It is connected via a serial port and is probably useless.

Private e-mail with Bill indicates his tape drive is connected to a
third party controller doing a MS: - emulation.  It might even accept
a TK25-compatible format.  

I have to agree, a TU58 is almost useless.  Backing up a 300 Mbyte
drive to one is patently insane!  (I know there are
fanatics out there - this is flame-bait for them!)

>It will support floppy drive, but do you have a controller for it.  I don't
>know RSTS, so I can't say whether or not it is likely that the driver you
>would need might already be on the system.

My recommendations would be:

1.  Figure out how to format cartridges for the existing tape drive.
    (This may even be a no-brainer.)  Will be a big win for backups,
    especially assuming someone can tell Bill how to generate a bootable
    RSTS/E restore utility tape.  (I have almost zero experience with RSTS/E
    of any version.)  I don't think RSTS/E has been distributed on
    TK25 for years, though, making upgrades difficult/impossible.

B.  Or, find a TQK50/TK50 tape system.  This will require a TMSCP
    driver (I don't know if this is a problem or not), but will be
    able to boot recent RSTS/E distributions and will be very useful
    for backups.  One of the few things I know about RSTS/E is that it
    will require late revisions of the TK50 hardware/firmware to work.
    In particular, there is a bipolar PROM on the TQK50 that will need
    to be labeled "065" and not "055".  This is a necessary, though
    perhaps not sufficient, condition, according to my experience.

    RSTS/E is still available on TK50.

III.Or, replace the existing drive and interface with a 9-track system
    and a TS11-compatible controller.  Perhaps a DEC TSV05 subsystem,
    or RSTS/E compatible 3rd-party hardware (again, a RSTS/E expert would
    have to step in and make a recommendation - I use Emulex TC02's
    all the time under RSX-11M+ and RT-11, but wouldn't be surprised
    if these are a problem under RSTS/E.)

    RSTS/E is also still available on 9-track.

Notice that I left out floppy drives as solutions to the backup/
distribution problem :-).  Almost as cruel as TU58's.  (I'd like
to see a TU58 advocate price the 1200 cartridges needed to back up
a 300 Mbyte disk drive!)

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



