> I have read that in Tokyo, building numbers in the order that buildings
> are built.  So the oldest buildings have the lowest numbers.  Adjacent
> numbers may be at opposite ends of a street.  Postal workers just have
> to know where things are.  Truth or fiction?
> -- 
> Carl Appellof

Truth.  Foobar Bldg 100 is right next to Foobaz Bldg 3, where 'Foobar', 'Foobaz'
is the name of some industrial group or other.  Only people who live in the
near neighborhood seem to know what is going on -- only the postman and the
local policeman seem to know where things are.  Even taxi drivers have to ask
the local policeman.  My cab driver drove around aimlessly looking for my
hotel (the Okura, next to the US Embassy), and although I could actually
see the top of the hotel, the cab couldn't seem to get any closer.  I finally
got out and walked home.

As I understand it, Gen. MacArthur had this grand plan to redevelop Tokyo
after WWII in a way that would make Tokyo look like Paris, Wash DC, and other
'planned' cities.  There was a lot of infighting about exactly what it was
supposed to look like, and I guess one night, people simply said 'scr*w it',
and just started building in a completely chaotic fashion.  I'm not sure of the
dates, but perhaps this was coincident with MacArthur getting fired.



