In article <58sb86$p01@news3.texas.net>, mrbill@staff1.texas.net says...
>
>Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
>: Private e-mail with Bill indicates his tape drive is connected to a
>: third party controller doing a MS: - emulation.  It might even accept
>: a TK25-compatible format.  

If it is close enough MS emulation, RSTS will handle it fine.

>: I have to agree, a TU58 is almost useless.  Backing up a 300 Mbyte
>: drive to one is patently insane!  (I know there are
>: fanatics out there - this is flame-bait for them!)
>: >It will support floppy drive, but do you have a controller for it.  I don't
>: >know RSTS, so I can't say whether or not it is likely that the driver you
>: >would need might already be on the system.
>: My recommendations would be:
>: 1.  Figure out how to format cartridges for the existing tape drive.
>:     (This may even be a no-brainer.)  Will be a big win for backups,
>:     especially assuming someone can tell Bill how to generate a bootable
>:     RSTS/E restore utility tape.  (I have almost zero experience with RSTS/E
>:     of any version.)  I don't think RSTS/E has been distributed on
>:     TK25 for years, though, making upgrades difficult/impossible.

At the dollar prompt ($), type 'INIT MS0: TEST/FORM=ANSI' and see
if it formats.

To create a bootable tape, enter the following at the init prompt
(prompt just after entering the date and time) 'COPY MS0'.
This will copy over a minimum system, with backup/restore
capability, and ability to copy back to the disk.

>: B.  Or, find a TQK50/TK50 tape system.  This will require a TMSCP
>:     driver (I don't know if this is a problem or not), but will be
>:     able to boot recent RSTS/E distributions and will be very useful
>:     for backups.  One of the few things I know about RSTS/E is that it
>:     will require late revisions of the TK50 hardware/firmware to work.
>:     In particular, there is a bipolar PROM on the TQK50 that will need
>:     to be labeled "065" and not "055".  This is a necessary, though
>:     perhaps not sufficient, condition, according to my experience.
>:     RSTS/E is still available on TK50.

Early versions of the TK50 would sometimes just hang, on both RSTS
and Vax/VMS, and the process using it would lock up and be unable
to be killed. Was both an OS and a firmware problem from what I know.
RSTS had a test added that it would refuse to work with out of
revision TK50 drives (got bit by this one myself)

>: III.Or, replace the existing drive and interface with a 9-track system
>:     and a TS11-compatible controller.  Perhaps a DEC TSV05 subsystem,
>:     or RSTS/E compatible 3rd-party hardware (again, a RSTS/E expert would
>:     have to step in and make a recommendation - I use Emulex TC02's
>:     all the time under RSX-11M+ and RT-11, but wouldn't be surprised
>:     if these are a problem under RSTS/E.)
>:     RSTS/E is also still available on 9-track.

I have a PDP11 11/83 (Q-Bus, RSTS/E) running a TSV05 just fine. It's a
1600bpi only tape drive. A TK50 tape holds more than a magtape so
TK50's may be a better bet (and they require less space)

>: Notice that I left out floppy drives as solutions to the backup/
>: distribution problem :-).  Almost as cruel as TU58's.  (I'd like
>: to see a TU58 advocate price the 1200 cartridges needed to back up
>: a 300 Mbyte disk drive!)
>: Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)

The RSTS/E distribution (at least up to 9.7 which is what I'm running)
fits on two 9-track tapes (1600bpi), or two TK50 tapes. You really
don't want to try to get it on floppy (A micro version existed at one
time on 20 RX50 floppies!)

>
>I'm *not* limited to RSTS/E - I just want to get this MicroPDP-11/23+ 
>running with *an* operating system so I can do some exploring, programming,
>etc.  However, getting *legal* OS media may be a bit of a problem.

You may want to look at Supnik's PDP11 emulator. He has images for
three versions of Unix which have been made publically available.
(System 5, System 6, and System 7)
They are RL02 images, so you may need to work them somewhat to get
another format. Don't know if they handle MSCP disks.

The simulator sources and documentation are contained in a compressed
tar archive on the public FTP server ftp.digital.com:

        /pub/DEC/sim/sources/sim_2.2d.tar.Z

The demonstration software and licenses are contained in multiple
compressed tar archives on the public FTP server ftp.digital.com:

        /pub/DEC/sim/software/uv5swre.tar.Z     - UNIX V5
        /pub/DEC/sim/software/uv6swre.tar.Z     - UNIX V6
        /pub/DEC/sim/software/uv7swre.tar.Z     - UNIX V7

>
>I'm probably gonna end up just junking the tape drive and will throw 
>in a floppy drive, unless someone has a better recommendation....
>
The only floppy I know that RSTS can handle is the RX50, a 400K
floppy that is really a strange piece of work. They may have made
others work, but I haven't used them myself. Plugs into the RQDX
controller.


