Michael S. Phillips wrote:
> 
> I am having difficulty comparing VAXes and Alphas.  We are looking to
> upgrade our VAX 4000-105a to an Alpha system, but I am unable to
> determine what Alpha system would give me equivalent performance.  All of
> DEC's comparison charts give VAX performance in terms of TPS and VUX but
> all Alpha systems are rated in terms of SPECint95 and I have no way to
> know if an Alpha with a 1.75 SPECint95 rating is equivalent to or better
> than a VAX that can do 181 TPS.  Can someone help me translate?
> --
> Michael S. Phillips
>   Systems Manager
>   MLS Hawaii, Inc.

Please note that in reading the following, there is NO promise made
that you can actually do the math which you will be tempted to do.
In fact, SPEC discourages you from doing so - see 

	http://www.specbench.org/osg/cpu95/news/cpu95qa.html#Q17

But anyway....

   1.  The VAX 11/780 was rated at 1.0 VUPs.
   2.  The VAX 11/780 was rated at 1.0 SPECint92.
   3.  The AlphaServer 2000 5/300 is rated at 319.3 SPECint92
   4.  The AlphaServer 2000 5/300 is rated at 8.02 SPECint95

For lots of SPEC95 ratings, visit the SPEC web site:

	http://www.specbench.org/cgi-bin/osgresults?conf=cpu95

The above is *the* official source of ratings.

For lots of SPEC92 ratings, you could try the incomplete and unofficial:

	http://netlib2.cs.utk.edu/performance/html/PDSbrowse.html   

For lots of VUPs ratings (again incomplete), read on.  I used to collect
VUP ratings, but stopped in the early 1990s.


    VUP Ratings as published in several Digital publications
    Send additions to henning@zko.dec.com (please cite date/name of
publication)

    NOTE: VUPs is an obsolete rating, and is not as clearly defined as
more
          modern rating systems (such as SPEC95).  


    S&O = Systems & Options Catalog

    DECdir = DECdirect

    SU = Sales Update

    

