In article <x47mmxvv4g.fsf@scooter.WPI.EDU>, Gregory Neil Shapiro
<gshapiro@wpi.edu> writes:

  For some background: DECtalk is wired and programmed and treated much
  like a serial printer.  But rather than outputting on paper, the DECtalk
  uses a speaker...  Programming the DECtalk is very similar to programming
  a serial terminal or a serial printer...  ASCII text, and ANSI escape
  and control sequences...

  Most of the problems I have seen with DECtalk have been due to incorrect
  serial line speeds, or due to incorrect serial line wiring.

:I am trying to get a DECtalk DTC01 (firmware version 2.0) to answer the phone
:and present a simple menu system.  Don't laugh, but I typed in the source from
:the Programmer Reference Manual, and of course, it fails.

  What host platform, what compiler, what compiler version, what failure(s),
  what error(s), what wiring, what communications serial port, what line
  speed, what power-up output, etc.?  (Just a few _minor_ questions... :-)

  Can you get the DECtalk DTC01 to operate as expected with a serial ASCII
  terminal attached to it?  (This is often the easiest way to program the
  DECtalk -- with the added bonus that an application can be used to `toggle
  off' the DECtalk and one can then `toggle on' the `display controls' option
  on the terminal, and then see exactly what got sent down the serial line.)

  Be aware that the DECtalk DTC01 does not have any out-dial call-progress
  capabilities -- it is capable of `blind dial' only.

:Does anyone have any functional software for this box?
 
  There is definitely a quantity of (functional) DECtalk software around:
  from Digital, from DECUS, from third-parties, etc.

