Ketil Z Malde <ketil@imr.no> wrote in article
<KETIL-ytqbuc45ezo.fsf@imr.no>...
> szoonem@astro.sunysb.edu (Saeid Zoonematkermani) writes:
> 
[snip]
> > - If NT4 runs on an Alpha box, where can I get the software? I know
> > one can
> >   buy an NT4 CD-ROM but I always assumed that it is for INTEL
> >   chips. So the question is whether there is a smart installer on the
> >   CD-ROM or one needs to get the right version to begin with?
[snip]

All shipping Windows NT CD-ROM:s have had the Alpha Intel and MIPS binaries
since the first few months after Windows NT 3.1 shipped (for the first few
months there Alpha binaries weren't there; the Alpha port started some 9
months after the MIPS port).

This means that on most Windows NT 3.1 and Windows NT Advanced Server 3.1
CD's and *ALL* Windows NT Workstation 3.5, Windows NT Server 3.5, Windows
NT
Workstation 3.51, Windows NT Server 3.51 and Windows NT Workstation 4.0,
Windows NT Server 4.0 CD's there are binaries for all supported platforms
(with the exception of PowerPC binaries, which started shipping on the 3.51
CD's).

And in the future the MIPS binaries will be dropped as Microsoft has
stated that MIPS won't be a platform where Windows NT is developed further.

In other words, take your Windows NT CD-ROM, and look in the \ALPHA
directory
for the Alpha binaries (\I386 for Intel, etc.).

