In article <E200vL.1L6@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
|>Thus far, no sharp-eyed sportscar enthusiast has solved the KryoTech 
|>Enigma, so I have another contest that will award the lucky winner with a 
|>three-month subscription to my newsletter.
|>
|>Today's topic is DIGITAL LOGOS. First person who can tell me just what the 
|>new Digital logo means is the Big Winner.
|>
|>To take a gander at the logo, head for the DEC home page. On the Investor 
|>Information and Digital Semiconductor pages you'll see a logo that looks 
|>for all the world like a funnel. Five cubes are falling into the top of 
|>the funnel; one is emerging from the base of the thing.
|>
|>What meaneth this icon?

It's not new and it's not a logo.  It's simply an icon indicating an
"InfoCenter" for our web pages.  We have a set of "design standards" for
Digital web pages, and that icon is part of them.  Also, it's been around
for at least a year, if not more.

My interpretation is that you are entering at the bottom into an area filled
with many different kinds of information, but the design standards call it
a "funnel icon", so maybe it's intended to evoke stuffing your head full of
InfoCenter info.
