In article <01bbe30c$aab93dc0$5982d9ce@demon.ix.netcom.com> "Bill Thomas" 
<bexpress@ix.netcom.com> writes:

>   Hi,
>I don't think I understood your post correctly.  So your saying..If you
>replace a P133 chip with a P150 chip in the same system...It makes perfect
>sense that it would run slower?  Can somebody help me out with this?
>                          Bill Thomas

I'm not a PC expert and don't know the specifics of the Starion systems.  
However, I've recently assembled my own PC systems from knowledge gained 
from reading through various PC hardware newsgroups.

In general, most Pentium motherboards run the systems bus, which connects 
the CPU, memory and PCI bus controller, at either 60 or 66Mhz.  The CPU 
clock speed and the PCI bus speed are obtained from clock muliplier or 
divisior circuits.

System Bus	CPU Speed (multiplier)		PCI speed (divisor)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
66Mhz		99 (1.5X)  = P100			33Mhz (/2)
60Mhz		120 (2.0X) = P120			30Mhz (/2)
66Mhz		132 (2.0X) = P133			33Mhz (/2)
60Mhz		150 (2.5X) = P150			30Mhz (/2)
66Mhz		165 (2.5X) = P166			33Mhz (/2)
60Mhz		180 (3.0X) = P180			30Mhz (/2)
66Mhz		198 (3.0X) = P200			33Mhz (/2)

The P150 (2.5 X 60) has a faster CPU clock speed than the P133 (2.5 X 66),
however, the system bus and PCI bus speed are running slower than the P133 
system. For compute intensive application the P150 system will out perform a 
P133 systems, but applications that are memory or I/O intensive will run 
faster on the P133 system.

