In article <E200vL.1L6@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>
>Today's topic is DIGITAL LOGOS. First person who can tell me just what the 
>new Digital logo means is the Big Winner.
>
>To take a gander at the logo, head for the DEC home page. On the Investor 
>Information and Digital Semiconductor pages you'll see a logo that looks 
>for all the world like a funnel. Five cubes are falling into the top of 
>the funnel; one is emerging from the base of the thing.
>
>What meaneth this icon?
>

Short answer - I have no idea, especially given the minute size and low
resolution of the image.

However, overall, it looks like that class of logos is a figment of the
web designer's imagination.  If you look at: 

  http://www.digital.com/info/digital-sitemap.html

and click on the different colored boxes you'll see that many of the blue
boxes in the lower half all have that logo, and that many others in the
web map shown also have odd but usually interpretable logos in the same
location on the page, and with similar color schemes. The logo attached to the
"directory" entry seems to imply that "you can get to many places from
here", and the "greetings" logo is an open door, "careers" shows a line of
faceless drones (doubtless the current corporate view of its employees),
ie, some of these things can be interpreted by mere mortals. It's possible
that not all of those odd logos are properly assigned.  That being said, it 
*looks* like each blue box under each green box in the map is supposed to
have the same logo (but the column under "partners" is the only
one 100% consistent with this theory). 

There is a logo index of sorts at:

   http://www.digital.com/info/pics/

Naturally, it doesn't explain what any of these new logos mean/are.

Back to the funnel logo.  Perhaps it indicates areas of the company soon to
suffer yet another round of massive layoffs (5 workers in, 1 worker out
:-( ). They put it on the financial pages too, since the downsizing is done
primarily to affect the company's short term bottom line, and hence its
stock value.  What I want to know is, where is the link to Digital
management, and what logo will go at the top of that page?  (This logo has
to properly symbolize management that seems incapable of reliably directing
employees into productive avenues, and so ends up firing them left and
right, doesn't know how to market products, etc., etc. ad nauseum.  Maybe
the logo should be a shepherd driving his flock off a cliff, or a sinking
ship with DIGITAL on the side???) 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



