> DEC's 1FQ97 Financials: Unintended Consequences
> 
> Copyright 1996 by Terry C. Shannon, Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC
> 
> It wasn't quite a repeat of the Ides of April 3FQ94 earnings
announcement, but DEC on October 22 released a 1FQ97 financial report t=
> hat looked far worse than anyone expected. Due largely to a sales force
reorganization, the firm posted a daunting $66M loss on shar=
> ply lower revenues. Paint it, bleak.
> 
> Symptoms and Side Effects
> 
> The numbers were downright ugly. A chastened Bob Palmer reported that
first quarter revenues were $2.91B, down from the $3.27B repor=
> ted for the comparable quarter a year ago. The 11 percent decline
reduced the firm's sales to their lowest level since 1987. The $66=
> M loss translates to $0.48 per share-three times the loss that Wall
Street analysts expected. Alpha product revenue growth screeched=
>  to a virtual standstill: Alpha sales grew by a mere 4 percent, down
drastically from the 38 percent year-over-year growth posted in=
>  4FQ96. Needless to say, Palmer was disappointed with the results. So
were the firm's stockholders: DEC stock dropped 5 3/8 points, =
> or 16 percent, on the bad news.
> 
> DEC ended the quarter with an employee population of 57,000, down 2,100
since the end of 4FQ96. This reduction is consistent with th=
> e firm's plans to end the fiscal year with a headcount of 53K. CFO Vin
Mullarkey said that DEC missed its quarterly revenue target b=
> y about 10 percent. Despite this, gross margin for the quarter was on
target at 31.3 percent. Product gross margin was up fractional=
> ly at 31.1 percent, while service gross margin declined, as expected, to
31.5 percent. 
> 
> Unix growth slowed to 9 percent, placing DEC behind the curve for the
first time in a dog's age. WNT-on-Alpha, however, was up more =
> than 100 percent year-over-year. Digital Semiconductor and the Digital
Services Division both achieved their quarterly goals, and DE=
> C experienced brisk growth in government sales. Significantly, channels
revenue grew by 14 percent during the quarter.
> 
> The DECline Diagnosed
> 
> While DEC's first quarter is traditionally weak, CEO Palmer attributed
the truly abysmal 1FQ97 numbers to the firm's recent salesfor=
> ce reorganization. No doubt. As we predicted last August ("DEC in FY96:
Three Steps Forward, One Step Back," SKD V3N15, August 3, 19=
> 96), the 'Maynard Musical Chairs' approach to employee reassignment did
in fact have serious side effects on productivity. As Bob pu=
> t it, the management team underestimated the distraction caused by the
changes in metrics, management, and account reassignment in t=
> he sales organization. (One telling example of the impact: sales of
margin-rich TurboLaser enterprise servers tanked because of disl=
> ocation in the enterprise sales force.) 
> 
> Regarding the reorg, the CEO admitted that "the price was greater than
we expected" and that Digital's "sales ability was temporaril=
> y reduced." But, claimed the CEO, "most of it is behind us." The latter
comment earned Mr. Palmer a very unflattering political cart=
> oon in the business section of the Boston Globe.
> 
> PCBU: Condition Upgraded
> 
> The PCBU was the whipping boy for DEC's setbacks in the third and fourth
quarters of fiscal 1996, but things are looking up at Bruce=
>  Claflin's Acton, MA headquarters. The PCBU doubled its gross margin
from 4FQ96 to 1FQ97, and remains "on track for break-even by th=
> e end of the calendar year." Inventory in the channels has been reduced
to less than 7 weeks, or by over $18M on a quarter-to-quarte=
> r basis. Aggregate PCBU revenues were down 17 percent in the quarter,
but server sales were up 27 percent. Total sales out were up 1=
> 0 to 14 percent across all three geographies. All in all, not a bad
accounting for the once-demonized PC business.
> 
> The Road to Wellville
> 
> It's clear that DEC has several daunting tasks in 2FQ97. First and
foremost is to return to profitability by the end of the calendar=
>  year. Mr. Palmer may enjoy the support of the Board of Directors, but
this support is likely to evaporate in the face of a third co=
> nsecutive losing quarter. Secondly, the firm must ramp up its marketing
efforts-not necessarily by spending more, but by spending sm=
> arter (blimps instead of chimps, for example). Third, DEC must continue
address the issue of revenue per employee. As shown in the c=
> hart, DEC must grow its revenue per employee by more than 20 percent
just to achieve parity with HP. It would take a boost of more t=
> han 60 percent to eclipse Sun Microsystems.
> 
> Of Cures and CEOs
> 
> Skeptics may claim that the DEC's current woes reflect the Palmer Method
writ large. And not entirely without justification: Two yea=
> rs ago the direct sales force was downsized. A year ago, it was
downsized again. Now the pendulum is swinging the other way. 1FQ97 b=
> egan and ended with the sudden departure of a senior-level manager, and
there's absolutely no doubt about who's behind the eight-bal=
> l today.
> 
> A Guarded Prognosis for Palmer
> 
> To be sure, the sales force 'dislocation' was largely responsible for
the 1FQ97 downturn. Dislocations are extremely painful, but ra=
> rely fatal to the patient. They can, however, be downright unhealthy for
the attending physician. If Doc Palmer fails to effect a cu=
> re for the problem by year's end, it's likely that the Board of
Directors will begin actively seeking a second opinion. Nevertheless=
> , DEC has been on the critical list before, and the patient certainly
responded favorably to the heroic measures prescribed by Palme=
> r and his team. As Mr. Palmer said during an interview with SKD, he
remains confident that DEC will be stabilized, revitalized and b=
> ack in the black by the end of the calendar year. If so, it's all but
certain that Bob will continue to make the rounds from his off=
> ice at 111 Powdermill Road
> 
