> Alpha Update: Faster, Better, Cheaper
> 
> (c) 1996 by Terry C. Shannon, Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC
> 
> While the trade press focuses on the latest RISC offerings from Sun and
HP, and on Intel's burgeoning presence in the server space, =
> Digital continues to up the Alpha ante. Although they have received
scant publicity, recent pricing and positioning refinements have=
>  significantly improved the competitive standing of the AlphaServer lineup.
> 
> Turbocharging TurboLaser
> 
> In an effort to render the top-of-the-line AlphaServer 8x00 5/440 more
attractive,  DEC reduced the cost of 5/440 SMP upgrades by 62=
>  percent. A 2-CPU module now goes for $65K. Not unexpectedly, the firm
reduced the price of entry-level AlphaServer 8200 5/300 syste=
> ms and memory by as much as 20 percent.
> 
> Midrange Manipulation
> 
> According to the latest DEC price book, AlphaServer 4000 and 4100 prices
have been reduced by as much as 25 percent. AlphaServer 400=
> 0 5/300 systems start at $30K, a reduction of 23 percent. A WNT-based
AlphaServer 4100 5/300 can now be had for $38K, or 25 percent =
> less than the system's original list price. Memory prices are down by as
much as 29 percent, reflecting a significant reduction in t=
> he cost of an average half-gigabyte Rawhide configuration.
> 
> Leveraging the Low End
> 
> Instead of phasing out AlphaServer 2000 and 2100A LP systems, DEC
provided an end-of-life performance kicker in the form of a 375 MH=
> z processor option. SKD anticipates near-term developments on the
AlphaServer 1000 front as well.
> 
> Thanks for the (Very Large) Memory
> 
> In conjunction with last month's AlphaServer 4000 introduction, DEC
announced a forthcoming twofold increase in the memory capacity =
> of the AlphaServer 4000 and 4100. Starting in November, the AlphaServer
4000 will support up to 4 GB of memory. AlphaServer 4100 use=
> rs will be able to configure their systems with 8 GB of memory.
> 
> Shortly thereafter, DEC will offer a 4 GB memory module for AlphaServer
8x00 enterprise servers. The new option will boost the memor=
> y capacity of AlphaServer 8200 and 8400 systems to 12 GB and 28 GB
respectively. Available in January, the board will be priced at l=
> ess than $100/MB.
> 
> Proof Points and Bragging Rights
> 
> The proliferation of the Alpha architecture was a consistent theme at
last month's Corporate Strategy Briefing. According to AlphaSe=
> rver maven Pauline Nist, TurboLaser and Rawhide continue to bring in the
big bucks. AlphaServer 8000 sales grew by 84 percent in 4FQ=
> 96, contributing to a 45 percent year-over-year Alpha revenue growth
rate. Based on an ASP in excess of $500K, TurboLaser has contri=
> buted over $1B to the Alpha revenue pot in less than 15 months. More
than 30 percent of the TurboLasers sold to date contain more th=
> an 2GB of memory, lending credence to the VLM64 value proposition.
> 
> In the benchmark department, Nist & Co. reasserted bragging rights for
midrange and high-end RISC systems. Delivering 7,598 tpmC at =
> $152/tpmC, the AlphaServer 4100 5/400 running Digital UNIX and Sybase
SQL Server System 11 is now the industry's top-performing four=
> -processor Unix SMP server.
> 
> Also announced were industry-leading single-system TPC-D results for a
12-CPU AlphaServer 8400 5/440 running Digital UNIX and Oracle=
> 7. Based on the TPC-D 100 GB database, the TurboLaser eclipsed the
previous record with a TPC-D Power rating of 864.3 QppD, Throughp=
> ut of 445.4 QphD, and Price/Performance of $2,170.37/QphD.
> 
> Yes, But...
> 
> It takes more than benchmarks and price cuts to sell enterprise-class
systems. If Digital can effectively complement its product enh=
> ancements with an enhanced and aggressive marketing strategy, Alpha
should continue its ascendancy.
> 
