szoonem@astro.sunysb.edu (Saeid Zoonematkermani) wrote onFri, 06 Dec
1996 16:51:51 -0500:

>- I looked at Microsoft web site to look for NT4 hardware compatibilities.
>  It does not list Alpha platform as a compatible configuration! I am confused
>  about this since I believe DEC sells Alpha workstation with pre-installed 
>  Windows NT. Is Microsoft web site wrong or does DEC do something funny?

Alpha Workstations most definitely are listed in the hardware
compatibility list (HCL). Please note that the HCL is organized by
processor architecture, and that the first (big) block of systems
listed are the x86-based PCs (followed by MIPS, Alpha, and PPC when I
last looked at it).

In fact, my AlphaStation 400 in the offices has two disks - it runs
Windows NT one day and OpenVMS the other day, depending on the work I
have to do.

>- If NT4 runs on an Alpha box, where can I get the software? I know one can
>  buy an NT4 CD-ROM but I always assumed that it is for INTEL chips. So the
>  question is whether there is a smart installer on the CD-ROM or one needs
>  to get the right version to begin with?

Each Windows NT CD-ROM has all hardware-specific versions on it. There
are /alpha, /i386, /mips and /ppc directories on it and you choose the
one corresponding to your hardware architecture.

>- How does one run applications that are written for NT on an Alpha chip? 
>  Generally when an application (e.g. Adobe Photoshop) is compiled to
>  produce the executable code, it will be for a specific CPU. Considering
>  that we live in an INTEL world, how can one run these NT applications on
>  other platforms? I can see two ways around this problem; one is that NT
>  would emulate an INTEL chip on other CPUs which should make it very slow,
>  and the other is that all the decent applications would have different
>  versions of the executable/binary code for different CPUs. 

There are two cases to consider:

- 16-bit Windows applications (from 3.x)
These are executed in Windows NTs 16-bit subsystem. As there was no
16-bit Windows on the RISC platforms, they only need to handle the
Intel code - and they do so.

- 32-bit Windows applications
Many software vendors ship native Alpha versions of their
applications. If your favourite application/tool/piece of shareware is
only available for Intel processors, there's help coming: the new
FX!32 product from Digital will translate Intel binaries on the fly
and keep translated versions of the most important modules on your
harddisk - ready to use when you invoke the application the next time.
Just check out 
     http://www.windows.digital.com/news/archives/961008fa.htp 
for more  information on FX!32.

And most important - FX!32 is free !  You can download it from 
     http://www.service.digital.com/fx32

>- Should I just forget about this and try to live with Linux?

You can also install Linux along with Windows NT and boot one or the
other depending on your needs.

>Since this is my first attempt in Windows world, any advice will be
>greatly appreciated. Thanks in Advance,

You're welcome.

