I don't know if this is any help, but we've successfully retrofitted
some real time equipment using an emulation so it's a doable thing. We
make an upgrade for a line of photolithography equipment based on the
PDP11/04 in which we replace the 11/04 with a software emulation and a
UNIBUS control cable. The original equipment used mag tape drives which
we emulate to eliminate the hassles of 9-track mag-tape files, and a
bunch of custom I/O cards in an expansion chassis which we control
through the UNIBUS cable. It operates in real time on a DOS PC at better
than the 11/04 speed (2-3x on a 486/dx66). Actually, the older versions
of this line used a PDP-8 controller for which we found it easier to
just convert the PDP-8 assembler to Pascal and replace the I/O with a
standard ISA-bus parallel I/O card. Either option might be open to you.

