carl@gerg.tamu.edu (Carl Perkins) wrote:

>In article <01bbe30c$aab93dc0$5982d9ce@demon.ix.netcom.com>, "Bill Thomas" <bexpress@ix.netcom.com> writes...
>}Hi,
>}	I don't think I understood your post correctly.  So your saying..If you
>}replace a P133 chip with a P150 chip in the same system...It makes perfect
>}sense that it would run slower?  Can somebody help me out with this?
>}			Bill Thomas

>In some cases, i.e. some applications/benchmarks, this will be true. Typically,
>P133 systems run a 66MHz bus (2x multiplier up to chip speed) but the
>P150 systems run a 50MHz bus (with 3x multiplier up to chip speed).  The
>buses are the same width, not surprising in the case of an "upgrade" since
>it is still the same bus just with a different jumper setting, so at 66MHz
>it will have a 32% higher throughput than at 50MHz.  Anything that is limited
>by the bus throughput will threfore be slower on the P150 than it was on the
>P133...

>Standard advice is to never get a P150, spend the extra $30 (or whatever it is)
>and get a P166 (which also uses the 66MHz bus, with a factor of 2.5 difference
>to chip speed).

>--- Carl





