I am a poor tech support schlep who has been handed a laptop he is not
familiar with (the brands I am familiar with are Dell, IBM, Compaq,
etc). laptop is a Digitial PC 486/SE running Windows for Workgroups.
Mission was to replace the current PCMCIA card (USR 14.4 modem) with a
XIRCOM combo ethernet/modem card, to save the guy from having to swap
the PCMCIA cards in and out when he plugs into our network. No sweat,
I think. I'm actually pretty knowledgeable about this stuff: would not
only say I've been around the blocks a few times, but also have been
dragged around them by the hair screaming

Of course I expected the usual problems - I/Os, IRQ's, getting W4WG to
see the network etc. I did not expect what happened: the laptop
STOPPED SEEING ITS HARDDRIVE.

On bootup, it now says, I seem to recall from this afternoon, "failure
to find harddrive 0" or something like that. The CMOS is fine; it sees
the 178 MG harddrive, and unfortunately pretending to make changes in
the CMOS saving it doesn't reset it. Wierd thing is, on bootup you can
boot from a floppy disk, and then see C drive just fine.

Normally, I would suspect perhaps that the PCMCIA card had grabbed
perhaps an I/O or an IRQ that the laptop used on bootup to refer to
the laptop. But bootup isn't even getting as far as the config.sys -
bootup starts to refer to the harddrive - you can hear that quiet
warming noise that harddrives begin as they start to spin, but then it
throws up that error message and offers you access to Setup (the
CMOS).

And, if you remove the PCMCIA card and try to boot, same results.

a)	any ideas, anyone?
b)	how can I find help on the digital site? I went to
http://www.windows.digital.com, but didn't find much to help. I am
more used to the very detailed and thorough sites at IBM and COMPAQ,
where many issues are documented and there are technical specs on
older models, but couldn't even find tech specs on this laptop at
digital. Did I miss them somehow?

the laptop is using the Phoenix Card and Socket Services. It is
running DOS 6.2 and W4WG 3.11. The RAM has not been upgraded; it is
still at 4 MEG.

Has something simply come loose inside the laptop? Some data cabling
perhaps? I have seen this in desktop models when the controller card
or a data cable is out of place.





