Terry C. Shannon wrote:
> Secondly, the products of decomposition produced by Halon 1401 (and the
> less user-friendly 1311) are toxic. Matter of fact, the first halon used
> for fire protection was carbon tetrachloride. Turns out that carbon tet
> decomposes to phosgene. After a few folks caught a snootful of this stuff
> and died, carbon tet extinguishers were banned. The only place you'll see
> 'em any more is in very old buildings, and then only very rarely. What you
> want to look for is a glass globe (usually filled with pink fluid)
> suspended from the ceiling. The globe has a nipple at the base, which
> ruptures in the presence of heat and allows the carbon tet to discharge.
> You happen to run across one o' these things, get rid of it.

The first time I encountered these (in fact, the *only* time) was at a
petrol station in Massachusetts.  There were these incredibly filthy
yellowish inverted-pear-shaped doobies hanging in wire cages (similar to
a wine bottle net) from the ceiling, and I was completely baffled.  I
nearly leapt out of my skin when, upon asking, I was told they contained
CCl4 - and I didn't know about the decomposition issue at the time.
Bad enough to be in a flaming petrol station without being doused with
carbon tet to boot.

This was in 1978, and I wouldn't be the least bit surprised to find them
still there.  Coated as they were with decades' worth of petroleum fume
condensation, they sorta added to the ambience and aesthetics of the
place.

#ken	:-)}



