Per Lindqvist <pgd@algonet.se> wrote:

> Boots up with uppercase only. Did unix really do that? I think that
> there used to be a test that if you only typed upper case, it switched
> automatically to upper-case-only mode, otherwise lower case mode
> default.

The tty driver in this copy of v7 is definitely very odd.  Notice that
single user mode, in addition to coming up in uppercase, has ^H as the
erase key, ^C as interrupt, and, insanely, ^Z as EOF.  So if you hit ^Z
at the single user login prompt and wait a few seconds, it'll come up
multiuser and you can log in normally, in lowercase, with DEL as
interrupt and ^D as EOF, since I guess login sets these itself.  I
think if you just press Return there without typing anything, it
prompts you again, this time in uppercase.

The tty driver is weird in other ways, too -- it's got ^W and ^R like
in Berkeley Unix, and if your erase key isn't ^H it does some weird
brackety thing when backspacing, like the /\ stuff you get in System V
and 4.4BSD with "echoprt" set, but with brackets instead.  Judging from
the comments in /usr/include/sys/tty.h, there is other weirdness too --
they've got ^P defined as some sort of pager feature, maybe like the
"cr3" mode that inspired the writing of "more."  I couldn't get that
one to work, though.

> No man pages, hmm.

But they are FTPable from bell-labs.com (sorry, don't remember the
exact URL) which means it's not a total loss not to have them on the
disk.  The v6 distribution does have man pages, though the man command
itself is missing.  You can read them by nroffing /mnt/man/man0/naa and
the manpage of your choice, then piping the result through something to
filter out extraneous control characters and backspaced underlining
that will otherwise make it look really nasty on a CRT.

You can tell I've spent *far* too much time playing with this the last
couple of evenings, eh? :)

> Anyways, nice of SCO to allow free usage of the binaries.

First the Lions book and now this!  Very nice of them.

Eric



