Backspace (control-H) as a delete key is very prevalent on MOST
        of the systems I have seen.(either default or settable.) Same
for
        control-C being  the break key, (many early systems had no easy
        way of detecting an actual ascii break,and quite a few terminal
        wouldn't actually send one,) and control-Z as EOF.

Bull.  As you went from system to system you got differing characters.
I remember using underscore (SHIFT-O on the teletypes) as the delete
character as well as RUBOUT on various machines.  HP's used Control-X
which teletypes frequenlty had listed as "CANCEL" as the line kill.
I've seen machines use ESC used for program delete.  Control-Z was
a feature of DEC OS's.  It might seem reasonable as DEC RT was the
model for CP/M which was the model for DOS.

Any of you who were APL afficianados knows there's a significant
difference between backspace and character delete.

        They have been around a long time before Unix was even ported
        to the PDP11, so the question of which interpertation is "odd"
        becomes an exercise in system bashing.

While an early UNIX was done on the PDP7, the first tolerable ones were
on PDP-11's so "port" is isn't really the operable word.

        I just wish, for old-times sake, the original framers of UNIX
        had tried to get along with the rest of the world, instead of
        always marching to the beat of a different drummer.

Actually, they were marching to the beat of the existing system.  The
UNIX tty characters follow that of the GCOS system these guys were
using.
At least they made it CONFIGURABLE (at least erase and kill)!

-Ron



