*Never* assume system perfromance is directly related to the CPU clock speed!

The are many factors that contribute to systems performance.  The CPU clock 
speed, like the number of cylinders in an automobile engine, is only one factor 
that contribute to over performance.  The performace ranges that you mention 
30-50% is not very surprising.  If fact, there is actually a good technical 
reason why a Pentium P150 would perform slower in some cases than a Pentium 
P133 systems.  Because the performance characteristic of any PC system is so 
complex, it is almost impossible to predict the performance of any PC system 
from the information supplied by the manufacture. 

 Luckily Ziff-Davis publication (PCWeek, Computer Shopper, etc.) and others 
available to consumers which publishing benchmarks. You might want to search 
there WWW site to see if they ever review any of the tarion models. 









