  As far as I can tell, it is not a problem with the hardware at all.
The dec unix console reads jumpers on the board to figure out what frequency
to send to the attached tga monitor, which in my case would be 64khz,
1280x1024x60hz.  Then, there is a switch to give to the eeprom to make
it send sync on green.  These two things result in a text mode in the srm
console that is functional on my fixed frequency monitor.  
  The nt/linux console, on the other hand (I believe it is called arc) 
seems to assume that the machine will have a monitor that works at least
in 31.5khz vga.  It uses this frequency for its output, regardless of 
the jumper settings.
  To make things worse, it would seem that even if one could persuade linux
to boot off the dec unix console in the proper video mode, linux itself
would reset the video card back to vga mode, unless specifically told 
not to in a command line switch or whatever.  I am not even sure if it is
possible that it can be told to output something different.  Certainly, even
if it can, it makes bootstrapping a multia to linux without a vga monitor
quite a chore.
  I find this whole situation quite silly.  They have managed to reinvent
all of the serious problems with vga on a system that doesn't support
a text mode at all.  Why the arc console doesn't read those jumpers is
beyond me.  
  Oh well, I shouldn't complain about the fit and finish on a free car,
right?
