In article <01bbd73e$33601660$ab8d93cf@400139425worldnet.att.net>, "A K" <akuebler@worldnet.att.net> writes:
:Is there a difference between 166MHz 64bit RISC Alpha CPU and a 166MHz
:Alpha AXP CPU?

  `AXP' was -- at one point in time -- intended to indicate an Alpha system
  that was from Digital and not from one of the other vendors shipping out
  Alpha systems.  Nothing more.

  Since `Alpha' generally refers to a family of microprocessor CPUs, there
  can potentially be some big differences in performance across families.
  Further, there are other factors that contribute to performance outside
  the microprocessor -- caches, bus bandwidth, etc., can all contribute to
  potential performance differences.

  As for general information on Digital, on OpenVMS, and on the meanings
  (or non-meanings) of `AXP' vs `Alpha' etc., please acquire and skim
  through the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) monthly posting.

