Silicon on Sapphire...
Er...as an ex-RCA-er, I must point out that the technique was one that
RCA pioneered and championed for many years. Ultimately the fab line cost
became too great to bear though, and RCA Semiconductor got dismantled
(along with its GE counterpart) after RCA was sold off to GE.)

SOS had considerable speed advantages when last seen over bulk CMOS
but fab at the time was more difficult than bulk. At that time, bulk
was "fast enough" for most users, and RCA's funds were being sucked away
by the effort (pushed by its then chairman) to get capacitive video disk
out the door. Obtaining capital for new fab lines in that environment is
extremely hard.

Which doesn't affect the possibility that using a substrate that conducts
heat well might allow speeds that currently cannot be handled. But you
do have to be able to produce the substrate at bulk, not jewelry, prices.



