In article <57vnpa$742@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>, geest3+@pitt.edu (Gregg E Economou) writes...
>In article <32A351B5.24C2@smarts.com>,
>Jerry Leichter  <leichter@smarts.com> wrote:
>>
>>A number of years back, someone - I think it was HP - developed an "SOS"
>>(Silicon On Saphire) technology.  They grew layers of silicon on top of
>>a saphire substrate.  As I recall, one of the advantages was that
>>saphire was a better thermal conductor than pure silicon.
> 
>this would work well, but you'd have to take extra steps to insulate electrical
>situations, since sapphire (Al2O3) conducts electricity better than pure 
>Si does, by a good margin... once you solved that problem, it seems liek it 
>would work well. And you can grow sapphire crystals almost any size you like, 
>and its probably cheaper in the long run (lower equipment cost i'd say) than
>Si. Funny how people dont alays do this kind of thing.. 
> 

Actually, Al2O3 (sapphire) is a very good insulator (much better than silicon).
Silicon on Sappire (SOS) eliminates the need for isolation junctions between
the active circuits and the substrate (sapphire vs. conventional silicon) and
reduces the capacitances, improving device speed.
The reason SOS did not succeed is that the added processing complexity (cost)
outweighed the speed increases.

Daniel Lang  dbl@hydra0.caltech.edu



