H.W. Stockman wrote:

> I saw an article in the early-to-mid 80's (probably
> in C & E News) that claimed ca. 50% of all U.S. CFC
> releases to the atmosphere were due to Halon
> fire-fighting agents.  There was a time when Halon
> was sprayed from firehoses, and used in vast amounts
> -- i.e. it was not just an ingredient in fire extinguishers.

I find this hard to believe.  The amount of Halon use can't
have possibly compared to the Freon leakage from air conditioners
and other consumer users.  The expense would have been staggering.

I don't know of any application where Halon was sprayed from
"fire hoses."   I was involved in Halon firefighting uses during
the entire 80's.

Halon is still the ideal use for enclosed habitable spaces like
airplane cabins.   Fortunately, it looks like it may finally get
an exemption for such (oddly, spraying Halon on a fire, renders
it ozone friendly).  A few risk management studies show that
Halon is actually *NOT* what you want in a central deluge system
as was in vogue in the 80's.  IBM stopped putting Halon in their
facilities as an unnecessary expense.  If you can't put the fire
out quickly with a hand extinguisher, you might as well use water
sprinklers.  More effective and much cheaper.

-Ron



