jfc@mit.edu (John Carr) wrote:
>In article <57usvk$a90$1@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>,
>Glenn C. Everhart <everhart@star.enet.dec.com> wrote:
>
>>Actually, if one could just build the chips on diamond substrates,
>>cooling should be quite simple. Diamond is a VERY good heat conducter.
>
>Didn't DEC do that for the VAX 9000?
>
Nope. The VAX 9000 relied on so-called High Density Signal Carrier (HDSC) 
multilayer, multichip modules. Leading edge technology, but it didn't rely 
on gleanings from DeBeers Consolidated mines. 

The 9000 had a big mother (about 2' x 2') metal plate called a Planar 
Module. The Planar Module could accommodate up to 16 HDSC modules. Certain 
versions of the Planar Module were equipped for water cooling, but DEC 
cancelled the Aquarius (water-cooled version) system and opted to release 
the Aridus (air-cooled version) instead. I'm told they cranked up the 
clock on the Aridus system to deliver the same 30 VUPS that was expected 
from the Aquarius.

For more information on the VAX 9000, try contacting Bob Glorioso at 
Marathon Computer Technologies, http://www.mial.com.

T'was a good product, but it was a day late and a dollar short. Within a 
year of the introduction of the 9000, DEC was getting equivalent 
performance out of the NVAX chips used in the VAX 6000 series. Still, the 
9000 incorporated mainframe-class availability and reliability adjuncts, 
as well as a nice crossbar switch architecture.

Terry Shannon
Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC





