DEC would probably hire you on the spot if you could tell them how to
make their 4GB memory boards out of commodity parts and still have
them work reliably.  You are talking over 2000 DRAM chips on a single
board if they're using 16MBit parts and well over 600 if they're
switching over to 64MBit (these aren't commodity yet).  And face it,
what you call commodity parts is often B- and C-rated output from the
DRAM manufacturers.  It may work in your PC, but would not in this
board.

