"H.W. Stockman" <hwstock@swcp.com> wrote:
>Terry C. Shannon wrote:
>
>> Very much off-topic, but the fire service also gains from the use of
>> halogenated hydrocarbons. I was a fire protection system designer back
>> when the CFC ban was imposed, and I couldn't for the life of me figure out
>> why Halon 1401 was not exempted from the ban.
>
>Again quite off-topic, but:
>
>I saw an article in the early-to-mid 80's (probably
>in C & E News) that claimed ca. 50% of all U.S. CFC
>releases to the atmosphere were due to Halon 
>fire-fighting agents.  There was a time when Halon
>was sprayed from firehoses, and used in vast amounts
>-- i.e. it was not just an ingredient in fire extinguishers.

C'est tres interessant, but I question the statistic. In Halon's defense, 
the stuff used to cost at least five bucks a pound (and subsequently 
increased to about $25.00 when the moratorium was imposed), making it 
prohibitively expensive for routine use. Secondly, in a perfect world, 
Halon would only be discharged in the event of a fire. If I were an 
occupant of a computer room that decided to host a nice little fire, I'd 
rather find myself in a 7 percent concentration of Halon (an atmosphere 
which makes you sound like Donald Duck, but otherwise is essentially 
harmless) than a 60 percent concentration of carbon dioxide (which 
supports neither combustion nor respiration.

I would argue that far more CFCs have leaked out of automobile air 
conditioners, home air conditioners, and decommissioned refrigerators than 
have been, or ever will be, released by fire suppression systems.

On the other hand, back in the old days an initial test of a Halon system 
was mandated by fire code. In other words, a system had to be activated 
and dumped to prove its efficacy. Instead of charging the system with an 
inert gas like nitrogen for the test, Halon was always used for this 
purpose. Dumb, no?

Secondly, the products of decomposition produced by Halon 1401 (and the 
less user-friendly 1311) are toxic. Matter of fact, the first halon used 
for fire protection was carbon tetrachloride. Turns out that carbon tet 
decomposes to phosgene. After a few folks caught a snootful of this stuff 
and died, carbon tet extinguishers were banned. The only place you'll see 
'em any more is in very old buildings, and then only very rarely. What you 
want to look for is a glass globe (usually filled with pink fluid) 
suspended from the ceiling. The globe has a nipple at the base, which 
ruptures in the presence of heat and allows the carbon tet to discharge. 
You happen to run across one o' these things, get rid of it.

As for Halon 1401 products of decomposition, the threshold value for 
toxicity is well above the threshold value for detection by the human 
respiratory system. In other words, the stuff is so irritating to the 
lungs, nose, and throat that you won't hang around long enough to get 
gassed.

And now we return to the regularly-scheduled business of this newsgroup!

Terry Shannon
Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC




